<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Strasbourg</h1><p class="st-meta">1262 CE – 1681 CE · capital Strasbourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The guild-run republic on the Rhine where Gutenberg experimented with printing and Martin Bucer led the Reformation, before Louis XIV annexed it in 1681.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of Strasbourg had long resented the rule of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. In 1262 they defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-of-geroldseck">Bishop Walter of Geroldseck</a> at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hausbergen">Hausbergen</a> and won self-government, and the city soon became a free city of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, outside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-strasbourg">Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1332 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> shared power with the patricians in a council and magistracy. Strasbourg traded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, finished its cathedral spire in 1439, and hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Johannes Gutenberg's</a> early work. It adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1520s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-sturm">Jacob Sturm</a> led its diplomacy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-sturm">Johannes Sturm</a> founded its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a>. The first printed newspaper appeared there in 1605.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1681, during peacetime, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV's</a> troops surrounded Strasbourg and forced it to surrender. It kept its Lutheran churches and local institutions, but became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> in 1697.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Strasbourg" width="320" height="254"><figcaption>Image: Buache, Philippe (1700-1773). Cartographe Delisle, Guillaume (1675-1726). Graveur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_de_France_divis%C3%A9e_suivant_les_quatre_d%C3%A9partements_de_Messieurs_les_secretaires_d%27Etat_-_par_Philippe_Buache_-_btv1b8593230j.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1262 CE – 1681 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Strasbourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacob Sturm</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Strasbourg, Strassburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1472&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Strasbourg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4860 480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Strasbourg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5180 736L5229 694M5180 736L5054 728M5180 736L5170 731"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0M5170 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Strasbourg. <a href="/atlas?year=1472&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
